## SOAP XXE Vulnerability in Spring Web Services

**The Problem**: Spring-WS uses XML parsing internally for SOAP message processing. Without explicit XXE protection, SOAP endpoints become XXE attack vectors.

**Real incident**: Enterprise integrations using Spring-WS for B2B SOAP exchanges were vulnerable to XXE attacks that:
- Allowed partners to read internal configuration files
- Enabled SSRF attacks to scan internal networks
- Crashed services via entity expansion (billion laughs)
- Exfiltrated sensitive data via blind XXE

## Vulnerable SOAP Endpoint

```java
@Endpoint
public class VulnerableSoapEndpoint {

    @PayloadRoot(namespace = "http://company.com/schema", localPart = "OrderRequest")
    @ResponsePayload
    public OrderResponse processOrder(@RequestPayload OrderRequest order) {
        // VULNERABLE: Spring-WS parses SOAP message without XXE protection
        // Attacker's SOAP body can contain XXE payload
        return new OrderResponse("Processed");
    }
}

@Configuration
@EnableWs
public class VulnerableSoapConfig extends WsConfigurerAdapter {

    @Bean
    public ServletRegistrationBean<MessageDispatcherServlet> messageDispatcherServlet(
            ApplicationContext applicationContext) {
        MessageDispatcherServlet servlet = new MessageDispatcherServlet();
        servlet.setApplicationContext(applicationContext);
        // VULNERABLE: No XXE protection configured
        return new ServletRegistrationBean<>(servlet, "/ws/*");
    }
}
```

**Attack**: Partner sends malicious SOAP request:
```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<soap:Envelope xmlns:soap="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/"
               xmlns:tns="http://company.com/schema">
  <soap:Body>
    <!DOCTYPE foo [
      <!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM "file:///etc/passwd">
    ]>
    <tns:OrderRequest>
      <orderId>&xxe;</orderId>
      <amount>1000</amount>
    </tns:OrderRequest>
  </soap:Body>
</soap:Envelope>
```

Server parses SOAP, expands entity, returns `/etc/passwd` contents in error or logs.

## Common Mistakes

1. **Blocklist instead of allowlist** — Trying to filter out dangerous characters (like `'`, `--`, `;`) instead of allowlisting expected input patterns. Attackers will always find encoding bypasses (URL encoding, Unicode, double encoding) that evade blocklists.

2. **Sanitizing at the wrong layer** — Applying sanitization in the frontend (JavaScript validation) instead of the backend. Client-side validation is trivially bypassed with curl, Burp Suite, or browser DevTools.

3. **Using string concatenation for queries** — Building queries with template literals or string concatenation instead of parameterized queries. Even "safe" values today can become attack vectors when code changes.

4. **Trusting ORM/framework defaults** — Assuming the ORM automatically prevents injection. Most ORMs have escape hatches (raw queries, text expressions) that bypass parameterization.

## Secure Implementation


### Tier 1: Critical Fix

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWs
public class SecureSoapConfig extends WsConfigurerAdapter {

    @Override
    public void addInterceptors(List<EndpointInterceptor> interceptors) {
        // Add XXE validation interceptor
        interceptors.add(new XxeValidationInterceptor());
    }

    @Bean
    public ServletRegistrationBean<MessageDispatcherServlet> messageDispatcherServlet(
            ApplicationContext applicationContext) {
        MessageDispatcherServlet servlet = new MessageDispatcherServlet();
        servlet.setApplicationContext(applicationContext);
        return new ServletRegistrationBean<>(servlet, "/ws/*");
    }

    @Bean
    public Wss4jSecurityInterceptor securityInterceptor() {
        Wss4jSecurityInterceptor interceptor = new Wss4jSecurityInterceptor();
        // Configure WS-Security policies
        return interceptor;
    }
}

@Component
public class XxeValidationInterceptor implements EndpointInterceptor {

    @Override
    public boolean handleRequest(MessageContext messageContext, Object endpoint) 
            throws Exception {

        // Get SOAP message
        SoapMessage message = (SoapMessage) messageContext.getRequest();
        Document doc = message.getDocument();

        // SECURE: Validate document for XXE patterns
        validateXxePatterns(doc);

        // SECURE: Validate document size and depth
        validateDocumentStructure(doc);

        return true;
    }

    private void validateXxePatterns(Document doc) throws SecurityException {
        DocumentType docType = doc.getDoctype();
        if (docType != null) {
            throw new SecurityException("DOCTYPE declarations not allowed in SOAP messages");
        }
    }

    private void validateDocumentStructure(Document doc) throws SecurityException {
        Element root = doc.getDocumentElement();

        // Max document depth
        if (getDepth(root) > 20) {
            throw new SecurityException("Document nesting too deep");
        }
    }

    private int getDepth(Element element) {
        int maxChildDepth = 0;
        for (Element child : getChildElements(element)) {
            maxChildDepth = Math.max(maxChildDepth, getDepth(child));
        }
        return 1 + maxChildDepth;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean handleResponse(MessageContext messageContext, Object endpoint) {
        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean handleFault(MessageContext messageContext, Object endpoint) {
        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public void afterCompletion(MessageContext messageContext, Object endpoint, 
                               Exception ex) {}
}

