## Summary

SOAP endpoints are high-value XXE targets because B2B partners often send XML. Secure by:
1. Validating DOCTYPE absence in SOAP messages
2. Validating document depth and size
3. Using proper Spring-WS security interceptors
4. Testing with XXE payloads

## Secure SOAP Attachments and MTOM/XOP

SOAP attachments are a common XXE vector because they're less commonly validated.

```java
@Configuration
public class SecureAttachmentConfig {

    @Bean
    public AttachmentInterceptor attachmentInterceptor() {
        return new AttachmentInterceptor();
    }
}

@Component
public class AttachmentInterceptor implements EndpointInterceptor {

    private static final long MAX_ATTACHMENT_SIZE = 10_000_000; // 10MB
    private static final Set<String> ALLOWED_MIME_TYPES = Set.of(
        "application/pdf",
        "image/jpeg",
        "image/png"
    );

    @Override
    public boolean handleRequest(MessageContext messageContext, Object endpoint) 
            throws Exception {

        SoapMessage request = (SoapMessage) messageContext.getRequest();

        // Validate SOAP attachments
        if (request instanceof SaajSoapMessage) {
            SaajSoapMessage saajMessage = (SaajSoapMessage) request;
            SOAPMessage soapMessage = saajMessage.getSaajMessage();

            // Get attachments
            Iterator<AttachmentPart> attachments = 
                (Iterator<AttachmentPart>) soapMessage.getAttachments();

            while (attachments.hasNext()) {
                AttachmentPart attachment = attachments.next();

                // Validate size
                if (attachment.getSize() > MAX_ATTACHMENT_SIZE) {
                    throw new SecurityException("Attachment too large");
                }

                // Validate MIME type
                String mimeType = attachment.getContentType();
                if (!ALLOWED_MIME_TYPES.contains(mimeType)) {
                    throw new SecurityException("MIME type not allowed: " + mimeType);
                }

                // For XML attachments, validate for XXE
                if (mimeType.contains("xml")) {
                    validateXmlAttachment(attachment);
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    private void validateXmlAttachment(AttachmentPart attachment) 
            throws Exception {

        InputStream stream = attachment.getRawContent();
        Document doc = parseXmlSecurely(stream);

        // Check for DOCTYPE
        if (doc.getDoctype() != null) {
            throw new SecurityException("DOCTYPE not allowed in XML attachment");
        }
    }

    private Document parseXmlSecurely(InputStream stream) throws Exception {
        DocumentBuilderFactory dbf = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
        dbf.setFeature("http://apache.org/xml/features/disallow-doctype-decl", true);
        dbf.setFeature("http://xml.org/sax/features/external-general-entities", false);
        dbf.setAttribute(javax.xml.XMLConstants.ACCESS_EXTERNAL_DTD, "");

        return dbf.newDocumentBuilder().parse(stream);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean handleResponse(MessageContext messageContext, Object endpoint) {
        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean handleFault(MessageContext messageContext, Object endpoint) {
        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public void afterCompletion(MessageContext messageContext, Object endpoint, 
                               Exception ex) {}
}

// Secure MTOM endpoint configuration
@Endpoint
public class SecureMtomEndpoint {

    @PayloadRoot(namespace = "http://company.com/schema", localPart = "DocumentSubmit")
    @ResponsePayload
    public SubmitResponse submitDocument(@RequestPayload DocumentSubmit request) 
            throws Exception {

        // MTOM/XOP automatically decodes binary content
        // Validate decoded content
        byte[] documentBytes = request.getDocument();

        // Validate file type
        String fileType = detectFileType(documentBytes);
        if (!isAllowedFileType(fileType)) {
            throw new SecurityException("File type not allowed: " + fileType);
        }

        // For XML files, parse securely
        if (fileType.equals("XML")) {
            validateXmlContent(documentBytes);
        }

        return new SubmitResponse("OK");
    }

    private void validateXmlContent(byte[] content) throws Exception {
        DocumentBuilderFactory dbf = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
        dbf.setFeature("http://apache.org/xml/features/disallow-doctype-decl", true);
        dbf.setFeature("http://xml.org/sax/features/external-general-entities", false);

        dbf.newDocumentBuilder().parse(
            new ByteArrayInputStream(content)
        );
    }

    private String detectFileType(byte[] content) {
        // Magic number detection
        if (startsWithBytes(content, "<?xml".getBytes())) return "XML";
        if (startsWithBytes(content, "%PDF".getBytes())) return "PDF";
        if (startsWithBytes(content, new byte[]{(byte)0xFF, (byte)0xD8})) return "JPEG";
        return "UNKNOWN";
    }

    private boolean isAllowedFileType(String type) {
        return Set.of("PDF", "JPEG", "PNG").contains(type);
    }

    private boolean startsWithBytes(byte[] content, byte[] prefix) {
        if (content.length < prefix.length) return false;
        for (int i = 0; i < prefix.length; i++) {
            if (content[i] != prefix[i]) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- Validate attachment MIME types (whitelist only)
- Validate attachment sizes (prevent DoS)
- Parse XML attachments with XXE protection
- Validate decoded MTOM/XOP binary content
- Detect file types via magic numbers, not extensions
- Test with malicious attachments and XXE payloads


## Lab Environment Setup

Reproduce and test XXE mitigation in isolation:

```bash
# Docker testbed with both vulnerable and hardened XML parsers
docker run -d --name xxe-lab -p 8080:8080 spring-xxe-testbed:latest
# Test that hardened parser rejects DOCTYPE declarations
curl -v -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/secure-xml -H "Content-Type: application/xml" -d '<!DOCTYPE foo [<!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM "file:///etc/passwd">]><data>&xxe;</data>'
# Expected: 400 Bad Request with "DOCTYPE is disallowed"
```

## Performance Considerations

XML parser hardening has negligible performance impact. Measured overhead:

- `setFeature(DISALLOW_DOCTYPE)`: <0.1ms per document — parser short-circuits immediately on DOCTYPE detection
- Schema validation adds 2-5ms per document for complex schemas — use only when business rules require it
- For high-throughput endpoints (>1000 req/s), consider StAX streaming parsers over DOM-based `DocumentBuilderFactory`

## Monitoring and Alerting

```java
// Log and alert on XXE attempt detection
@Component
public class XxeDetectionFilter implements Filter {
    private final MeterRegistry meterRegistry;

    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest req, ServletResponse res, FilterChain chain) {
        HttpServletRequest httpReq = (HttpServletRequest) req;
        String contentType = httpReq.getContentType();
        if (contentType != null && contentType.contains("xml")) {
            meterRegistry.counter("security.xxe.xml_request").increment();
        }
        chain.doFilter(req, res);
    }
}
```

## Security Testing Checklist

- **SAST scanning**: Run Find Security Bugs rule `XXE_DOCUMENT` to detect unsafe `DocumentBuilderFactory` configurations
- **DAST validation**: Use OWASP ZAP XXE scan policy against all XML endpoints
- **Penetration testing**: Test blind XXE via out-of-band techniques (DNS exfiltration) using Burp Collaborator


## Prerequisites

You should understand XML parsing fundamentals (DOM vs SAX vs StAX), how Document Type Definitions (DTDs) enable entity expansion, and Spring's `Jaxb2Marshaller` configuration before implementing XXE prevention controls.
